> #### *** THE RULE'S ADVICE WAS RIGHT AND ITS EXEMPLAR WAS THE TREE'S ONE OUTLIER.
> #### Corrected 2026-08-25 -- and it was corrected BY FOLLOWING THE RULE. ***
>
> The rule says *grep the fork for the existing idiom rather than reason from first
> principles*. The first round to obey it did exactly that, on `wdcflood`, and the census
> came back against the rule's own example.
>
> **`memory_rw.c:495-523` is a bounded diagnostic, but it is the ONLY counter-style bound in
> the tree, and every property that makes it a bad model was verified by reading it:**
>
> * it is **not a `fatal()`** -- `debugmsg_cpu(..., VERBOSITY_DEBUG, ...)`;
> * it is **DOUBLE-GATED**: `ENOUGH_VERBOSITY(SUBSYS_MEMORY, VERBOSITY_DEBUG) && n_trunc_msgs < 8`,
>   and because `&&` short-circuits, **the counter never increments at default verbosity**. The
>   bound almost never engages, because the message is already invisible;
> * `static int n_trunc_msgs` is function-local -- **GLOBAL, shared by every device and CPU**,
>   not per-instance;
> * it prints **no give-up notice**.
>
> > **THE HOUSE IDIOM IS NOT "FIRST N THEN QUIET". IT IS `N = 1`, PER DEVICE INSTANCE, PLUS AN
> > EXPLICIT SUPPRESSION MARKER IN THE MESSAGE TEXT** -- roughly 34 latch sites across 11 device
> > files. So member 3 should be read as **DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE**, and the counter form is
> > the exception that needs a warrant, not the template.
> >
> > *** THE MARKER'S SPELLING WAS NAMED WRONG HERE: this clause used to say the idiom is
> > `(further occurrences suppressed)`, which is the MINORITY form. Censused 2026-08-26 across
> > `src/devices/*.c`, by message text: ***
> >
> > *** ALL THREE NUMBERS WENT STALE AND WERE RE-DERIVED 2026-08-30, after the kimi seat
> > challenged the `(once)` figure in a brief that quoted this block.  It counted 24 where
> > this said 9, and it was right.  The fable5 seat independently confirmed 24 and supplied
> > the reason: *the count has grown past 9 because `#497` itself added six sites.* ***
> >
> > **THE DERIVER, so the next reader re-runs it instead of trusting a literal:**
> >
> >     for m in '(once)' 'further occurrences suppressed' 'reported once'; do
> >         printf '%-34s %3s occ %3s files' "$m" \
> >             "$(grep -o "$m" src/devices/*.c | wc -l)" \
> >             "$(grep -l "$m" src/devices/*.c | wc -l)"; echo; done
>
> *** AND THAT DERIVER IS KEYED ON A CORRELATE.  MEASURED 2026-09-17: THE LITERAL SEES 41 OF 71.
> Found by the glm and minimax seats challenging a brief that had inlined its output as SETTLED. ***
>
> **A latch marker is defined by ANNOUNCING SUPPRESSION, not by the four characters `(once)`.**  The
> literal misses 23 occurrences in QUALIFIED spellings -- `(once per offset)` 11, `(once per bit)` 5,
> `(once per offset per direction)` 4, plus `(once per encoding)`, `(once per direction)`,
> `(once for each channel)` -- and SEVEN MORE that span a WRAPPED C STRING, `#453`'s own
> `"... (once per"` / `"channel) ..."` among them.  **The `(once...)` family is ~90% of all markers,
> not the 82% the literal supports.**
>
> ### *** AND THAT 90% WAS ALSO WRONG.  THE TRUE FIGURE IS **51%**, AND THE FAMILY REGEX MISSED IT
> ### IN **BOTH DIRECTIONS AT ONCE**.  Measured 2026-09-17 by the Opus 5 seat, reproduced here. ***
>
> **80 markers across 12 files, THIRTEEN distinct spellings.**  `(once)` is 41 of 80 -- **51%**.
> Not the ~82% the three-spelling count gave, and not the ~90% the family regex gave.
>
>     (once) 41   (once per offset) 12   (further occurrences suppressed) 5   (once per bit) 5
>     (once per encoding) 5   (once per offset per direction) 4   (reported once) 2
>     (once for each channel) 1   (once per direction) 1   (once per channel) 1
>     (once per timer) 1   (reported once per cpu) 1   (reported once per register) 1
>
> **TWO INDEPENDENT CAUSES, PUSHING OPPOSITE WAYS -- which is why every single-method count so far
> has been wrong, and why they disagreed without converging:**
>
> * **LINE-WRAP BLINDNESS UNDERCOUNTS the qualified family.**  This tree wraps C string literals at
>   80 columns, so a marker split across a continuation (`"... (once per"` / `"channel) ..."`) is
>   invisible to any single-line grep.  **JOIN ADJACENT STRING LITERALS FIRST** -- and the join must
>   be `"\s*"` (quote, ANY whitespace, quote), NOT a newline-requiring form: this tree also has
>   same-line adjacent literals.
> * **SUBSTRING MATCHING OVERCOUNTS `(reported once)`** by folding in `(reported once per cpu)` and
>   `(reported once per register)`, which are DIFFERENT spellings.
>
> **THE DERIVER, VERIFIED TO REPRODUCE 80/13/51% BEFORE BEING WRITTEN HERE:**
>
>     JOIN = re.compile(r'"\s*"', re.S)
>     MARK = re.compile(r'\((?:once|further occurrences suppressed|reported once)[^)]*\)')
>     for f in glob.glob(".../src/devices/*.c"):
>         for m in MARK.findall(JOIN.sub("", open(f, encoding="utf-8").read())): ...
>
> *** THE SPELLING COUNT IS THE SELF-CHECK, AND IT IS WHY A WRONG JOIN IS CATCHABLE: a join that
> FAILS leaves FRAGMENTS that masquerade as new spellings.  The right join gives **13**; a
> newline-requiring join gives 79 markers and **17** -- the four extra "spellings" are torn
> markers.  If your spelling count is above 13, your join is broken, not the tree. ***
>
> **AND THE ARTIFACT `census.py` CANNOT BE RUN AS SHIPPED FROM git-bash**: it globs a `/mnt/c/...`
> WSL path, so Windows python matches NOTHING and it prints `TOTAL markers: 0 across 0 files` --
> a clean, confident, wrong answer, which is this file's own pattern-that-selected-nothing class.
> Point it at the Windows path or run it under WSL.  Preserved at
> `regress/artifacts/seataudit_20260917/opus_measured_20260917/census.py`.
>
> *This is the FIFTH generation of this number (14, 24, 40, 41-as-82%, 80-as-51%) and the THIRD
> correction in a single day.  The ADVICE has survived every one of them: `(once)` is the right
> default for an UNQUALIFIED latch.  Only the arithmetic keeps rotting -- and the reason it rots is
> that every method so far measured a CORRELATE of "is this a suppression marker" rather than the
> thing itself.*
>
> **RUN THE FAMILY FORM BESIDE THE LITERAL, and mind which tool you use:**
>
>     grep -ohE '\(once[^)]*\)' src/devices/*.c | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn   # LINE-BASED: 64
>
> `grep` is line-based and CANNOT see a marker split across a string continuation.  Python's
> `[^)]*` matches newlines, so `re.findall` over whole-file text spans them and reports 71.  **The
> two disagree by exactly the wrapped markers; that is a result, not an artifact.**
>
> *This block has now been re-derived FOUR times (14, 24, 40, 41), each run confirming the drift and
> none questioning the KEY -- which is this file's own rule landing on this file: a correlate's error
> is invisible because the output is still a plausible number.  The ADVICE is unchanged and
> strengthened again: `(once)` is what a new site should use.*
