                       anchors -- which is the rule this list exists to enforce.  `21066_66a_hrm.txt:8840'
                       (S6.2.6) says CPU physical address bits <31:7> map to PCI address bits <26:2>, i.e. THE
                       SHIFT OF 5 the fork's `relative_addr >> 5' implements; `:9216' (S6.2.9) says PCI ad<1:0>
                       come from IOC_CONF<1:0>, so IOC_CONF selects the CYCLE TYPE and does NOT supply the bus
                       number; and `:9486' (S6.2.18, PCI Master Abort) says an unanswered read "returns
                       arbitrary data with error status to the CPU, which causes an instruction exception",
                       plus an internal interrupt and ERR/CODE/CMD/ADDR logging.  *** THAT LAST ONE REFUTES A
                       PREMISE THIS PROJECT HAD BEEN REASONING FROM: absent-device reads on real LCA hardware
                       are NOT all-ones.  The 0xffffffff.. signature is an EMULATOR-COMPATIBILITY oracle
                       (`bus_pci_data_access' returns it for register 0 ONLY, zero for every other register),
                       never a hardware contract. ***  `file` reports this PDF as "10 page(s)" and that is
                       WRONG -- the real count is in a linearized trailer, and `pdftotext -layout` yields
                       713 KB / 13,876 lines.  An LCA/Alpha-PCI round opens THIS first.)
  ddi0406.pdf         (ADDED 2026-09-08: ARM Architecture Reference Manual, ARMv7-A/R edition, ARM DDI
                       0406C.d (ID040418), 18.6 MB -> `ddi0406.txt' 8.4 MB / 143,260 lines.  *** THIS IS
                       NOT ddi0100i.  THE OLDER ONE PREDATES ARMv6T2 AND CONTAINS NO BFI, BFC, UBFX OR
                       SBFX AT ALL -- `grep -c -w BFI ddi0100i.txt' is ZERO, which is how two ARM rows
                       came to be blocked for want of a source that was simply the wrong manual. ***
                       Fetched at the GPT-6 Astra seat's citation on this list's own vmesbcp2 and 21066
                       precedents, and then VERIFIED LOCALLY rather than taken from the seat's page
                       anchor -- THREE candidate URLs of mine had returned JSON or HTML and only the
                       seat's object id served the PDF.  `ddi0406.txt:18066' (A8.8.20, BFI) reads
                       `if msbit >= lsbit then R[d]<msbit:lsbit> = R[n]<(msbit-lsbit):0>; // Other bits
                       of R[d] are unchanged  else UNPREDICTABLE;' and `:17995' (A8.8.19, BFC) is
                       identical in structure.  So `msbit < lsbit' is UNPREDICTABLE WITH NO REQUIRED
                       DESTINATION VALUE -- preserving the destination is an emulator POLICY choice and
                       may not be described as architecture -- and the VALID case is constrained by
                       "other bits unchanged", which is what a mask must respect.  `<width>' is "in the
                       range 1 to 32-<lsb>", so width 32 implies lsb 0 and copies the whole register.
                       An ARM bitfield round opens THIS, never ddi0100i.)
  ppc_uisa_book1_v201.pdf
                      (ADDED 2026-09-10: PowerPC User Instruction Set Architecture, Book I, Version
                       2.01, September 2003 -- IBM, Wetzel/May/Frey/Silha.  997 KB -> 784 KB of text,
                       12,586 lines.  Fetched WITH THE OWNER'S APPROVAL on this list's own vmesbcp2 /
                       21066 / ddi0406 precedent, and then VERIFIED LOCALLY rather than taken from a
                       seat's page anchor.  *** IT IS THE DOCUMENT THE 2005 PEM NAMES AS DEFINING OVER
                       ITSELF: ppc_pem_32and64_2005.txt:302 reads "This book does not attempt to
                       replace the PowerPC Architecture specification (version 2.01) ... which remains
                       the defining manual for the PowerPC Architecture." ***  It was fetched to settle
                       a question the four other local PowerPC manuals could not -- whether rlwinm with
                       a WRAPPING mask (MB > ME) writes the rotated word into the destination's HIGH
                       half.  `:4125-4137' gives the RTL, r <- ROTL32((RS)32:63, n); m <- MASK(MB+32,
                       ME+32); RA <- r & m, and `:578' defines MASK as "1s in positions x through y
                       (WRAPPING IF x > y) and 0s elsewhere".  *** AND THE DECIDING FACT IS AN ABSENCE:
                       BOOK I's CLEARING SENTENCE IS **SCOPED** AND THE PEM'S IS **UNCONDITIONAL**,
                       and that difference is the whole finding.  Book I `:4195-4196' says "For all
                       the uses given above, the high-order 32 bits of register RA are cleared" --
                       qualified to the programming-note uses enumerated just above it, every one of
                       which fixes a NON-WRAPPING (MB, ME).  The PEM carries that same qualified
                       sentence at :30074 AND an UNCONDITIONAL one at :30065, inside its instruction
                       description.  *** IT IS THE UNCONDITIONAL CLAIM THAT IS ABSENT FROM BOOK I,
                       and neither qualified sentence reaches the wrapping case, so Book I's normative
                       RTL governs it. ***
                       *** AND READ TO :4200, NOT :4190 -- I FIRST WROTE THIS ENTRY CLAIMING BOOK I
                       "NEVER SAYS" IT, HAVING READ :4170-4190 AND STOPPED FIVE LINES SHORT OF THE
                       SENTENCE. ***  The entry already told the next reader to check the absence by
                       READING rather than by one grep; that instruction was right and my execution of
                       it was not.  **An absence claim is only as good as the span you actually read,
                       and the span must be chosen to CONTAIN the thing you are denying, not to end
                       where the interesting text seems to stop.**  The conclusion was unaffected --
                       both PowerPC replacements verified against Book I afterwards -- which is
                       exactly why the wrong sentence survived a first reading.  Its left column on the same page also gives
                       rldic's mask as MASK(b, !n), which corroborates the !n = 63-SH complement that
                       the rldimi work depends on.  `file' reports "10 page(s)" and is WRONG, exactly
                       as it was for 21066.  A round on ANY PowerPC rotate-and-mask instruction opens
                       THIS before the PEM.)
```

